\DOC LOCAL_MUL_SAFETY_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_MUL_SAFETY_TAC : string -> int -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Uses the subroutine {ensures} specification labelled {assump_name} in the
assumption list to take one call step, advancing the symbolic state from index
{n} to index {n+1}.

\DESCRIBE
{assump_name : string} is the label of the assumption holding the universally
quantified {ensures} specification of the called subroutine, used to take the
call step. {n : int} is the index of the current symbolic state, so the goal's
state variable is advanced from the one indexed {n} to the one indexed {n+1}.
The conclusion, an {eventually} statement about the current state variable,
becomes the same {eventually} statement about the next state variable. All
assumptions describing the old state — program-counter, register, flag and
memory reads, the event-trace equation, the cumulative frame/{MAYCHANGE}
relation and the {bytes_loaded} fact — are removed and replaced by the
corresponding assumptions about the new state, with an updated frame relation
and an event-trace equation phrased in terms of a freshly introduced trace
variable {x1 ... xn} (one new variable per application), together with that
variable's defining equation and an accompanying memory-access-bounds
assumption; some reads present before (for instance ones about registers
overwritten by the call) are simply dropped. Assumptions unrelated to the
state, including the specification named by {assump_name} and the other
quantified subroutine specifications, survive, although their order in the list
may change.
{
       A ?- t
       ==================================  LOCAL_MUL_SAFETY_TAC assump_name n
       A' ?- u
}
\noindent where {A'} is {A} with the old state's facts replaced by the new
state's facts plus the equation and bounds assumption for the new trace
variable, and {u} is {t} with the state variable advanced.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single kind of outcome, always on goals
whose conclusion is an {eventually} statement at a subroutine call site with a
large body of state assumptions present.

\ENDDOC
